  "id": 4809,
  "slug": "top-10-events-of-2014",
  "title": "Top 10 Events of 2014",
  "content": "<p><em>28th December 2015</em></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"></p>\n<p>We here at The Fix live a charmed life. </p>\n<p>Going to see amazing shows and performances for free, that we would happily have sold our gran to get into, is a true blessing.</p>\n<p>Thus, it is with a certain level of guilt, and perhaps even sheer embarrassment, we feel it is necessary to remind you of some of the wonderful things we have witnessed this year.</p>\n<p>So here they are, most definitely in no particular order:</p>\n<p><strong>Juno &amp; The Paycock, Bristol Old Vic &#8211; 11<sup>th</sup> September<br /></strong><a href=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/juno-and-the-paycock/\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"alignleft wp-image-3678 size-medium\" src=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/09/Juno-and-the-Paycock-Des-McAleer-as-Capt-Boyle-Picture-by-Stephen-Vaughan-1-300x200.jpg\" alt=\"Juno and the Paycock  -prod shot 1\" width=\"300\" height=\"200\" srcset=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/09/Juno-and-the-Paycock-Des-McAleer-as-Capt-Boyle-Picture-by-Stephen-Vaughan-1-300x200.jpg 300w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/09/Juno-and-the-Paycock-Des-McAleer-as-Capt-Boyle-Picture-by-Stephen-Vaughan-1-675x450.jpg 675w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/09/Juno-and-the-Paycock-Des-McAleer-as-Capt-Boyle-Picture-by-Stephen-Vaughan-1-120x80.jpg 120w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px\" /></a>2014 was well under way when we were floored by this hysterical and touching reimagining of Sean O&#8217;Casey&#8217;s Irish classic. Gemma Bodinetz directed a phenomenal cast &#8211; highlighted by Niamh Cusack &amp; Des McAleer &#8211;<strong> </strong>but this was also a story brought to life by those behind the scenes.<strong> </strong>Conor Murphy<strong> </strong>(stage design) gave us a true masterclass in set design and Peter Coyte (composer) provided the exquisite soundtrack. Heartbreaking and heartfelt this was an incredible achievement. <strong>(KM)</strong></p>\n<p>Read our original review <a href=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/juno-and-the-paycock/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>.</p>\n<p>\n<iframe loading=\"lazy\" title=\"Juno and the Paycock Trailer\" width=\"640\" height=\"360\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/1f7qDzUuC-A?feature=oembed\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share\" referrerpolicy=\"strict-origin-when-cross-origin\" allowfullscreen></iframe></p>\n<hr />\n<p><strong>John Grant, Colston Hall &#8211; 25<sup>th</sup> November<br /></strong><a href=\"http://johngrantmusic.com\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"alignleft wp-image-4930 size-medium\" src=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/12/John-Grant-008-300x180.jpg\" alt=\"John-Grant-008\" width=\"300\" height=\"180\" srcset=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/12/John-Grant-008-300x180.jpg 300w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/12/John-Grant-008-200x120.jpg 200w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/12/John-Grant-008.jpg 460w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px\" /></a>John Grant is a Norse God for modern times, a bear of a man dedicated to banishing his own demons through heartfelt songwriting. Whether tackling depression, his previous drug habit, the homophobic abuse he suffered growing up or his battle with HIV, the potency of his lyrics never fail to wrench the heart. Combining his synth-heavy compositions with the Royal Northern Sinfonia at Colston Hall brought the audience, at points, as close to a Nietzschean euphoria as possible within the confines of a music hall. A clichéd hack would call the show spine-tingling and hair-raising, and would have every right to do so. <strong>(CD)</strong></p>\n<p>Find out more <a href=\"http://johngrantmusic.com\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>.</p>\n<p>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dDN5H4pjFLg</p>\n<hr />\n<p><strong>Dylan Thomas: Return Journey, Tobacco Factory &#8211; 23<sup>rd </sup>Sept<br /></strong><a href=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/dylan-thomas-return-journey/\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"alignleft wp-image-3871 size-medium\" src=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/09/46413-300x197.jpg\" alt=\"46413\" width=\"300\" height=\"197\" srcset=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/09/46413-300x197.jpg 300w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/09/46413-120x80.jpg 120w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/09/46413.jpg 683w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px\" /></a><span class=\"TextRun SCX238608498\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX238608498\">I</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX238608498\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX238608498\">n an almost chillingly accurate</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX238608498\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX238608498\"> portrayal of the late poet’s US lecture tours of the 1950s, ultra-talented actor Bob Kingdom spellbindingly </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX238608498\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX238608498\">recreated</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX238608498\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX238608498\"> the loquacious </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX238608498\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX238608498\">bril</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX238608498\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX238608498\">liance of the debauched</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX238608498\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX238608498\"> Welshman during two consecutive nights at the Tobacco Factory. Uncannily capturing both Thomas’ physical and verbal dexterities, Kingdom</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX238608498\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX238608498\"> flawlessly</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX238608498\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX238608498\"> regaled the audie</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX238608498\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX238608498\">nce with an 85 minute monologue of surrealist whimsy, lengthy storytelling and recitations of so</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX238608498\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX238608498\">me of Thomas’ most famous poems</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX238608498\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX238608498\"> without missing a</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX238608498\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX238608498\"> beat</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX238608498\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX238608498\">. The excellence of a linguistically dense</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX238608498\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX238608498\">, authentic</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX238608498\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX238608498\"> and amusing script, taken from Thomas’ writings and public readings, was second only to Kingdom’s utter</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX238608498\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX238608498\">ly</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX238608498\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX238608498\"> convincing embodiment of Thomas’ spirit; as mesmerising a one man performance as you’re likely to see. </span></span><strong>(SH)<br /></strong></p>\n<p>Read our original review <a href=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/dylan-thomas-return-journey/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>.</p>\n<p>\n<iframe loading=\"lazy\" title=\"Dylan Thomas reads &quot;Do Not Go Gentle Into That Good Night&quot;\" width=\"640\" height=\"480\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/1mRec3VbH3w?feature=oembed\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share\" referrerpolicy=\"strict-origin-when-cross-origin\" allowfullscreen></iframe></p>\n<hr />\n<p><strong>Catfish and the Bottlemen, Louisiana – 25<sup>th</sup> February<br /></strong><a href=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/catb/\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"alignleft wp-image-4584 size-medium\" src=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/12/JS45318405-300x199.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"300\" height=\"199\" srcset=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/12/JS45318405-300x199.jpg 300w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/12/JS45318405-200x133.jpg 200w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/12/JS45318405.jpg 615w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px\" /></a>After being awarded ‘Best Newcomer’ at the recent BBC Music Awards, Catfish and the Bottlemen is a band that are very much in vogue. Their debut album <em>The Balcony</em> came out in September and their latest tour of the UK was a near sell-out, including the gig at Bristol’s Thekla in December. BUT, whilst they weren’t as polished 10 months earlier, their performance at Lousiana was a special night, made partly by frontman Van McCann’s banter and storytelling (Stealing from Travelodges nationwide and Big Jeff’s preference of Fleet Foxes et al) between their youthful, heartfelt and very British indie rock. <strong>(AD)</strong></p>\n<p>Read our original review <a href=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/catb/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>.</p>\n<p>\n<iframe loading=\"lazy\" title=\"Catfish and the Bottlemen - Pacifier live @ Bristol Thekla 4/12/14\" width=\"640\" height=\"360\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/-ikqg_FhBSw?feature=oembed\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share\" referrerpolicy=\"strict-origin-when-cross-origin\" allowfullscreen></iframe></p>\n<hr />\n<p><strong>Liz Green, Grain Barge &#8211; 24th April<br /><a href=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/liz-green/\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"alignleft wp-image-4931 size-medium\" src=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/12/liz-green-int2014-feb636-380-300x179.jpg\" alt=\"liz-green-int2014-feb636-380\" width=\"300\" height=\"179\" srcset=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/12/liz-green-int2014-feb636-380-300x179.jpg 300w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/12/liz-green-int2014-feb636-380-200x119.jpg 200w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/12/liz-green-int2014-feb636-380.jpg 636w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px\" /></a></strong><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX245917243\">A cosy Thursday evening of i</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX245917243\">ntimate exclusivity aboard the </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX245917243\">unsung</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX245917243\"> younger brother of Bristol’s two floating music venues, the Manchester based singer-songwriter’s appearance at The Grain Barge was one of the year’s most unexpected delights. Swaying through a myriad of </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX245917243\">old fashioned </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX245917243\">styles ranging </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX245917243\">fr</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX245917243\">om traditional folk, blues and </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"SpellingError SCX245917243\">Bossa</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX245917243\"> </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX245917243\">nova tinged jazz, every moment was an expression of Green’</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX245917243\">s quirky artistry and taste for anachronism. Aside from her musical gifts, Green’s unfeigned and effortless in-between s</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX245917243\">ong</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX245917243\"> badinage is s</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX245917243\">o amusing as to sometimes give </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX245917243\">off the vibe of</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX245917243\"> an established comedienne performing</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX245917243\"> a handy little side-line in music.</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX245917243\"> Within the seated, candle-lit intimacy of a sparsely filled venue, Green’s all round ability to entertain </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX245917243\">provided the most charming of spring evenings for the select few</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX245917243\">, </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX245917243\">fortunate</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX245917243\"> or judicious </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX245917243\">enough</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX245917243\">,</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX245917243\"> to </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX245917243\">have </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX245917243\">be</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX245917243\">en</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX245917243\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX245917243\"> in attendance.</span></span><span class=\"EOP SCX245917243\"> </span><strong>(SH)</strong></p>\n<p>Read our original review <a href=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/liz-green/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>.</p>\n<p>\n<iframe loading=\"lazy\" title=\"Liz Green - Bad Medicine (OFFICIAL VIDEO)\" width=\"640\" height=\"360\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/ojMlySunFY8?feature=oembed\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share\" referrerpolicy=\"strict-origin-when-cross-origin\" allowfullscreen></iframe></p>\n<hr />\n<p><strong>The Libertine, Bristol Old Vic &#8211; 29<sup>th</sup> May<br /><a href=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/the-libertine/\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"alignleft wp-image-3108 size-medium\" src=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/06/1_The-Libertine_blogspot-1500x1000-300x200.jpg\" alt=\"1_The Libertine_blogspot 1500x1000\" width=\"300\" height=\"200\" srcset=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/06/1_The-Libertine_blogspot-1500x1000-300x200.jpg 300w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/06/1_The-Libertine_blogspot-1500x1000-120x80.jpg 120w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/06/1_The-Libertine_blogspot-1500x1000.jpg 673w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px\" /></a></strong>A subtly realistic portrayal of the indulgences of court life and with all the wit, flair and bawdiness of a Restoration Comedy, this brilliant play was both a historical romp and incisive critique of excess. Martin Hutson&#8217;s superb performance as John Wilmot, 2nd Earl of Rochester, was given with a true punk sensibility, that the Earl himself would no doubt have endorsed. The life and times of the rakish, and ultimately self destructive Libertine have never been explored so beautifully. <strong>(KM)</strong></p>\n<p>Read our original review <a href=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/the-libertine/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>. </p>\n<p>\n<iframe loading=\"lazy\" title=\"The Libertine Trailer\" width=\"640\" height=\"360\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/5F0yLqO8jaU?feature=oembed\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share\" referrerpolicy=\"strict-origin-when-cross-origin\" allowfullscreen></iframe></p>\n<hr />\n<p><strong>The Orwell&#8217;s, Fleece– 21<sup>st</sup> November<br /></strong><a href=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/the-orwells/\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"alignleft wp-image-4454 size-medium\" src=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/11/TheOrwells_1-300x199.jpg\" alt=\"TheOrwells_1\" width=\"300\" height=\"199\" srcset=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/11/TheOrwells_1-300x199.jpg 300w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/11/TheOrwells_1-675x450.jpg 675w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/11/TheOrwells_1-200x133.jpg 200w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/11/TheOrwells_1.jpg 950w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px\" /></a>Fuelled by a jubilant Friday night atmosphere of callow pre-drink inebriation and smuggled liquor bottles, The Orwells’ sell out show at The Fleece was surely one of our fair city’s most riotous gigs this year. Following a palpable, pre-show buzz of tipsy expectation, the Chicago band’s boisterous choruses and sleazy garage rock riffs provided the perfect soundtrack to this hedonistic end of week celebration of youth. Continuous crowd surfing, frequent stage invasions, occasional attempts to climb the venue’s reinforcement beams and one instance of an audience member delivering lead vocal duties, all made for a thrilling evening of reckless abandon. A joyous spectacle of a youthful band in harmony with a room of similarly young fans, the enjoyment of this gig was as much to do with the crowd’s reaction to the music as it was the music itself. <strong>(SH)</strong></p>\n<p>Read our original preview <a href=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/the-orwells/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>.</p>\n<p>\n<iframe loading=\"lazy\" title=\"The Orwells. Mallrats / Who Needs You. The Fleece, Bristol. Nov 21, 2014\" width=\"640\" height=\"360\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/OOeFlxOLsTY?feature=oembed\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share\" referrerpolicy=\"strict-origin-when-cross-origin\" allowfullscreen></iframe></p>\n<hr />\n<p><strong>The Godney Gathering, Near Glastonbury – 21<sup>st</sup> July<br /></strong><a href=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/gatheringreef/\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"alignleft wp-image-4928 size-medium\" src=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/12/2-1-300x200.jpg\" alt=\"2-1\" width=\"300\" height=\"200\" srcset=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/12/2-1-300x200.jpg 300w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/12/2-1-675x450.jpg 675w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/12/2-1-200x133.jpg 200w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/12/2-1-120x80.jpg 120w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/12/2-1.jpg 720w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px\" /></a>A one day festival on the rise, actually situated closer to Glastonbury Tor than Michael Eavis’s summer shindig, the excellent and affordable Godney Gathering returned for its 4<sup>th</sup> year with REEF headlining the biggest of four stages. These local heroes spiked huge interest in the event, but there was a fantastic array of acts spanning genres with Gaz Brookfield, UK:ID and Nick Parker and the False Alarms putting in monumental performances, whilst new acts like Jess Handley, Lauren Cooper and Finches Fly made encouraging festival debuts. Each ‘Gathering’ sees local music supported and money raised for local causes. These events are important. <strong>(AD)</strong></p>\n<p>Read our original preview <a href=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/gatheringreef/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>.</p>\n<p>\n<iframe loading=\"lazy\" title=\"GODNEY GATHERING 2014 best bits - vlog\" width=\"640\" height=\"360\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/4kM7Zw9OMJ8?feature=oembed\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share\" referrerpolicy=\"strict-origin-when-cross-origin\" allowfullscreen></iframe></p>\n<hr />\n<p><b>Dracula, Bristol Old Vic &#8211; <strong>2<sup>nd</sup> October<br /></strong></b><a href=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/dracula-bristol-old-vic/\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-3915 size-medium alignleft\" src=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/10/10-Dracula-Credit-Farrows-Creative-300x200.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"300\" height=\"200\" srcset=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/10/10-Dracula-Credit-Farrows-Creative-300x200.jpg 300w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/10/10-Dracula-Credit-Farrows-Creative.jpg 460w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px\" /></a>Following a great reception at the Tobacco Factory in 2013, Marc Bruce&#8217;s <i>Dracula</i> returned to Bristol to the grander of the Old Vic. A sinister, restless adaptation of Bram Stoker&#8217;s tale of the Count, the dance piece emerged sulphourous and pulsating from a stunning stage set. Blending traditional with interpretive dance in a throbbing narrative, <i>Dracula</i> managed to convey a rich dynamic of emotions. The audience was left absorbed and breathless in a gothic stupor, quite a feat for a performance lacking a single piece of dialogue. <strong>(CD)</strong></p>\n<p>Read our original review <a href=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/dracula-bristol-old-vic/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>.</p>\n<p>\n<iframe loading=\"lazy\" title=\"Dracula by Mark Bruce Company\" width=\"640\" height=\"360\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/RqUPk2Udw8s?feature=oembed\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share\" referrerpolicy=\"strict-origin-when-cross-origin\" allowfullscreen></iframe></p>\n<hr />\n<p><b>David Thomas Broughton, Grain Barge &#8211; <strong>24<sup>th</sup> September</strong><br /></b><a href=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/gig-review-david-thomas-broughton-grain-barge/\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"alignleft wp-image-3885 size-medium\" src=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/09/5787223140_5db2b217f1_z-300x200.jpg\" alt=\"5787223140_5db2b217f1_z\" width=\"300\" height=\"200\" srcset=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/09/5787223140_5db2b217f1_z-300x200.jpg 300w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/09/5787223140_5db2b217f1_z-120x80.jpg 120w, https://www.thefixmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/09/5787223140_5db2b217f1_z.jpg 640w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px\" /></a>Not only one of the highlights of 2014, but one of the finest gigs The Fix has ever witnessed. Showcasing his album <i>Sliding the Same Way</i> with collaborators Juice Vocal Ensemble, David Thomas Broughton&#8217;s gig was a masterpiece of understated theatricality. From floating with candles amongst the cosy crowd, to tangling themselves in microphone cables and improvising percussion out of teacups, Juice gave Broughton the perfect foundation to loop his elegant guitar playing and ethereal voice. At one point flicking The Fix&#8217;s quiff while in full lyrical flow, Broughton and Juice created a gig as intimate as a Sunday snuggle and as memorable as music can ever be. <strong>(CD)</strong></p>\n<p>Read our original review <a href=\"https://www.thefixmagazine.com/gig-review-david-thomas-broughton-grain-barge/\">here</a>. </p>\n<p>\n<iframe loading=\"lazy\" title=\"David Thomas Broughton - Staying True\" width=\"640\" height=\"360\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/ST4apmPjwso?feature=oembed\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share\" referrerpolicy=\"strict-origin-when-cross-origin\" allowfullscreen></iframe></p>\n<p>&#8212;&#8212;&#8212;&#8212;&#8212;&#8212;&#8212;&#8212;&#8212;&#8212;&#8212;&#8212;&#8212;&#8212;&#8212;&#8212;&#8212;&#8212;&#8212;&#8212;&#8212;&#8212;&#8212;&#8212;&#8212;&#8211;</p>\n<p>And there you have it.</p>\n<p>Roll on 2015!!</p>\n<p><strong>By Kevin McGough (KM), Conal Dougan (CD), Arran Dutton (AD),  and Scott Hammond (SH). </strong></p>\n<p></p>",
